      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-324233",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $\\lambda (4a+\\sqrt{7}b)$ and $4\\sqrt{7}\\,a+7b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n4\\sqrt{7}\\,a+7b=\\lambda (4a+\\sqrt{7}b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{7}(4a+\\sqrt{7}b)=\\lambda (4a+\\sqrt{7}b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(4a+\\sqrt{7}b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=4a+\\sqrt{7}b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{7}\\,u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =\\sqrt{7}.$"
    },
