      ":question/id": "a4c754c3-d143-5941-8c38-1a8c75e6be25",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-20395",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $\\lambda (12a-52b)$ and $-3a+13b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n-3a+13b=\\lambda (12a-52b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}(12a-52b)=\\lambda (12a-52b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(12a-52b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=12a-52b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{4}u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{4}.$"
    },
