      ":question/id": "b26b5414-ab10-5d8a-9dff-43df60b1ebb9",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-20398",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $\\lambda (\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{7}{2}b)$ and $4a+49b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $\\lambda .$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Since the vectors are equal, we can write\n\n$$\n4a+49b=\\lambda (\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{7}{2}b).\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the left-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n14(\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{7}{2}b)=\\lambda (\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{7}{2}b).\n$$\n\nNotice that we have $(\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{7}{2}b)$ on both sides. So, let's define a new vector $u$ as\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{7}{2}b.\n$$\n\nThen, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n14u=\\lambda u.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =14.$"
    }
